<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Birinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 650</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Saint Birinus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Birinus was a missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> I who converted the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. In 635 he baptised their king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynegils">Cynegils</a>, with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a> as godfather, and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorchester-on-thames">Dorchester-on-Thames</a> as the seat of his bishopric. He worked among the West Saxons until his death around 650 and was venerated as a saint, the apostle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birinus.jpg" alt="Birinus" width="309" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: StephenPaternoster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Birinus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
